<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- MT-02: server-address trust for the WebView.

     Base (what a release APK ships): the platform's system CAs, plus cleartext — the app
     connects to user-entered plain-HTTP LAN addresses (http://<lan-ip>:5001), and
     declarative config cannot scope to private ranges, so cleartext is permitted as with
     the old usesCleartextTraffic="true". That is the product, not a gap.

     The dev-only trust anchors are in <debug-overrides> (ST-90, public-demo gate 5):
       - @raw/test_root_ca: the build-host .test root CA (public cert only, the key stays
         on the box) — trust in https://<name>.test with no per-device install.
       - user: user-installed CAs, i.e. whatever a proxy tool (mitmproxy/Charles) or a
         device-admin profile puts in the phone's store.
     Trusting those in a RELEASE build is the risk: a user-installed CA — installable by
     any app that asks, or by a phishing profile — can then read the bearer token on the
     wire, and the bundled CA's key lives on one dev machine. A debug build (android:
     debuggable=true) keeps both, so development, the .test hostnames and proxy
     inspection work exactly as before. The APK published by Mobile/publish-apk.sh is a
     debug build; a release build is the store/official artifact and gets the narrow set.

     Rotating the dev CA = ship a new debug APK (in-app update), as before. -->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <debug-overrides>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
            <certificates src="@raw/test_root_ca" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
